  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Friday)\nIssued at 142 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nBy Sunday morning, we should see widespread showers ongoing across\nmost of the region, with areas right along the Rio Grande missing\nout on the higher rain chances. Rainfall amounts should pick up in\nthe afternoon and evening as strong forcing for ascent moves into\nsouth central Texas. We also expect to see the leading edge of the\ncold front working southward into our region. Based on good run-\nto-run consistency noted in the ECMWF AI and several other models\ntrending this direction, we have again lowered maximum\ntemperatures on Sunday for areas generally along and north of a\nLlano to Austin to La Grange line. At least one of the hi-res\nmodels is also trending this direction and with high rain chances\nand cold air advection, there is opportunity to lower temperatures\nfarther to the south. SPC maintains a low risk (level 1 of 5) for\na strong storm or two for areas east of I-35/south of I-10 on\nSunday. Suspect much will depend on the speed of the low-level\ncold air advection, but we certainly can't rule out a strong storm\nor two near the coastal plains region. \n\nBeginning Sunday night, the upper level system will slide eastward\nand shift the higher rain chances into southeast Texas. Gusty\nnortheast winds will filter in as cold air advection increases.\nMonday should remain dry and cold for most areas, although a\nlingering shower could occur along and east of the Highway 77\ncorridor. Rainfall amounts for the weekend should remain lowest\nalong the Rio Grande, where up to 0.10\" is expected. Farther east\ninto the Hill Country and along the I-35/I-37 corridors, some\nfairly widespread 1\" amounts are forecast. Amounts increase to\nnear 2\" over the coastal plains and Highway 77 corridor. \n\nHigh temperatures on Monday will be much cooler with gusty\nnortheast winds and plenty of cloud cover keeping highs down in\nthe the lower 50s to lower 60s. We may need to go cooler with our\nforecast highs in the Hill Country pending later model data. Cool\ntemperatures linger into Tuesday as northeast winds begin to ease.\nWinds will trend to a more east to southeasterly direction for\nWednesday and Thursday as broad, but fairly weak southwest flow\naloft sets up. This in addition to the subtropical jet near of\njust south of the region will yield some low end (20-40%)\nprecipitation chances for most areas through Friday.",
